--named after Hastin, son of Suhotra; see हास्तिनपुर;
1: भा. IX. २१. २०; वा. ९९. १६५; Vi. IV. १९. २८.
sacred to ललीटा pi1t2ha;
2: Br. IV. 4. ९७.
sacred to Jayanti;
3: M. १०३. १४.
washed away by the Ganges floods.
4: Vi. IV. २१. 8.

--(also हस्तिनापुर and गजाह्व);
1: भा. I. 4. 6; 8. ४५. १०. 7; १३. 1;
visit- ed by कृष्ण often. Here कृष्ण spent some months after भीष्म's death and युधिष्ठिर's coronation. A Brahmana of this city informed कृष्ण of the banishment of the पाण्डवस् to forest;
2: Ib. I. 9. ४८; X. ५८. 1; ६४; 2-4; ७१. २२; ७५. ३८ [2]; ८० [2]; ८० [5]; ८४. ६९ [3].
visited by अक्रूर,
3: Ib. X. ४८. ३२; ४९. 1.
and by बलराम. The latter got offended at the remark of the Kurus about his king and threatened to lift up the city and throw it into the Ganges.
4: Ib. X. ६८. (whole).
Return of the पाण्डवस् and others after visit to Syamanta पञ्चक; visited by कृतवर्मन्
5: Ib. X. ५२ [५६ (v) 3] [१३].
and by सत्यभामा after her father's death;
6: Ib. X. ५७. 8.
left by Vidura;
7: Ib. III. 1. १७; IV. ३१. ३०.
when this city was washed away कौशाम्बी became capital;
8: Ib. IX. ३२. ४०.
capital of the Kurus and परीक्षित्.
9: Ib. I. १७. ४४; Vi. V. ३५. ३२.
